#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7657ff9-3918-5080-b9a2-8f6a6d8b4fd9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Homogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1d14f74-b428-5857-8ab6-52aa8c2d9612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163401} {:answer/id #uuid "356b9c26-475c-5a95-b06a-87e02aebbcc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163402} {:answer/id #uuid "48d3ccd0-e9f5-5405-9735-d346424da8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163403} {:answer/id #uuid "54c675e4-c3b3-5012-8b11-e18bfaea8555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163404} {:answer/id #uuid "7a08d94d-f8b5-5c8b-a9de-20435080c638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48d3ccd0-e9f5-5405-9735-d346424da8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163403} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdc1b479-dbe5-562c-8ba4-f9856a5ab051" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6028a3d5-97f7-5eca-bdb0-193599d89649" :question/math-academy-id "q-107937" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{y}^{′}-y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd0d3599-6297-59ce-8a64-c99e931a4330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8(2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380407} {:answer/id #uuid "adc5b24c-fc24-5d47-b284-477597052b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380408} {:answer/id #uuid "5fee1643-0534-59f5-a028-0f614a9bbcda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380409} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ad7f3e-4506-5467-b6a0-c04078ff1bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380410} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4c0797-21eb-5f1e-9ea3-8f99dee48abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16({x}_{1}+4{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1ad7f3e-4506-5467-b6a0-c04078ff1bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380410} :answer-field/id #uuid "725311ef-1f3d-5db4-adca-0f73a6abd290" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b332ac9d-e1aa-5efa-b417-32a7d962209b" :question/math-academy-id "q-109635" :question/problem "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-8{y}^{′}-16y=0,\\,y(1)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & ={x}_{1}(1)=3 \\\\ {y}^{′}(1) & ={x}_{2}(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=8{y}^{′}+16y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=8{x}_{2}+16{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(1)=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=16{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=3,q=1,f(t)=16{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p+q)\\cdot f(t) & =(3-1)(16{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =2(8)(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =16(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "771c8137-7dd9-5758-bb0a-ffc6dc52d469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12(3{x}_{1}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380414} {:answer/id #uuid "82b63a8a-38e3-5ba5-bd8a-89d58d436f7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380415} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6d2c00-369a-5efc-b831-acbe6746bb70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12(3{x}_{1}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380416} {:answer/id #uuid "839f1a25-3851-5a20-af1e-3a5c6d4af041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380417} {:answer/id #uuid "a35bcfa7-f0d1-5df7-a71d-27c1b0711d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a35bcfa7-f0d1-5df7-a71d-27c1b0711d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380418} :answer-field/id #uuid "9497cbb4-0690-57c4-8ade-12f260987f50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14da67e4-ca85-5189-99d0-c71c3051d763" :question/math-academy-id "q-109636" :question/problem "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-10{y}^{′}+25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,y=0,\\,y(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=10{y}^{′}-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=10{x}_{2}-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=-1 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=-1,q=2,f(t)=-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(-1-2)(-25\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3(-5)(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =15(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053bf2a5-aa50-57aa-85b2-20427a62051d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380421} {:answer/id #uuid "2d46168f-7a0f-5a78-a880-016a6fa26e4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380422} {:answer/id #uuid "92641ae3-ff7f-5ada-b77c-5049af5554ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380423} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4714dc-008f-5ac2-a284-b33923b09bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380424} {:answer/id #uuid "a920502b-11cb-572d-9404-2a028ce878c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c4714dc-008f-5ac2-a284-b33923b09bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380424} :answer-field/id #uuid "9479ba64-af75-5aa3-8f16-b5502d6effe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d721395-7f60-58c6-8c94-e984d75af312" :question/math-academy-id "q-109644" :question/problem "The third-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=4\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{3}(0)=r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ ${x}_{3}(t)={y}^{″}(t),$ and $p,$ $q$ and $r$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q-r)\\cdot f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{3}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=-1 \\\\ {y}^{″}(0) & ={x}_{3}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{‴}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3}, \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}={y}^{‴}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{‴}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{y}^{″}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{‴}=-{x}_{3}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}^{′}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3},\\, & {x}_{3}(0)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=-1,r=4,f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q-r)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-(-1)-4)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(2+1-4)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(-1)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7657ff9-3918-5080-b9a2-8f6a6d8b4fd9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Homogeneous ODE as a Matrix Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6d18008-e207-53a1-ba40-56d636e8c410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4{t}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380428} {:answer/id #uuid "1acd58de-5aa6-51de-a3c9-9bb6e738a243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -5t & 4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380429} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cc7177-6b1e-5e45-84e6-95d9a0050f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4{t}^{3} & 5t\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380430} {:answer/id #uuid "f5eb5139-cba2-5ac2-a1e8-d7c9a5e36ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5t & 4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380431} {:answer/id #uuid "35e54c5c-1bd8-5865-98fa-50638187c3bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & t \\\\ 4{t}^{3} & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5eb5139-cba2-5ac2-a1e8-d7c9a5e36ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5t & 4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380431} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa4c4df5-9c6d-5917-9761-8b56610953b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a927c15-49d0-5bb3-a29e-68798842c0ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-109691" :question/problem "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-4{t}^{3}{y}^{′}-5ty=0$\ncan be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\\,y(t)={x}_{1}(t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nFind the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′} & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{t}^{3}{y}^{′}+5ty.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{t}^{3}{x}_{2}+5t{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=5t{x}_{1}+4{t}^{3}{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5t & 4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence, $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 5t & 4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}].$"}]}
